FerrionOS change to upstream RedoxFS (MIT license, kept), made against upstream commit 5ba45a6887374ab425326656a276bb3b62eba7dc after the existing recipe patches. Checked by the deterministic simulation in sim/redoxfs (sim/redoxfs/run.sh maps SEEDS OPS, which also fails disk writes during commits) and by scripts/vm-test.py shm-reuse and shm-read-back.

A shared mapping of a file gets the pages of a buffer RedoxFS keeps for the file's node: the kernel lends the client those pages. The buffer is a page cache in src/page_cache.rs, built for every target so the simulation drives it. A range is read from the disk when it is first mapped, with zeros after the end of the file.

write() copies into every cached page it overlaps and a size change zeroes the cached bytes between the old and the new end, both only once the transaction has committed (PageCache::apply after FileSystem::tx returns), so a failed commit leaves the cache as the disk is; read() takes the bytes of pages a shared writable mapping may have changed from the cache, munmap and fsync write cached pages up to the end of the file and no further, and a node's cache is unmapped and dropped when its last handle and mapping are gone. One node's mappings are bounded by MAX_MAPPED_BYTES and MAX_MAPPED_RANGES (ENOMEM), its handles by MAX_NODE_HANDLES (EMFILE), and a map or unmap of no bytes or at an offset that is not a page boundary answers EINVAL: an empty unmap inside a mapped range made the range tree loop for good.

